[100/1873] metaoverfit-paper
TITLE: Overfitting in Published Meta-Regressions: Simulation and Empirical Survey
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: R-squared optimism
DATA: 18 simulation configs, 16 published meta-regressions
PATH: C:\Projects\metaoverfit-paper

CURRENT BODY (156 words):
How frequently do published meta-regressions operate in statistical regimes where apparent explanatory power is substantially inflated by overfitting? We combined simulation experiments across 18 parameter configurations with an empirical survey of 16 published meta-regressions from the metadat package spanning medicine, education, and domains. The metaoverfit R package computed LOOCV-corrected R-squared alongside bootstrap confidence intervals for each benchmark, testing whether reported explanatory power survives internal validation. Mean calibration optimism was 0.31 (95% CI 0.13 to 0.49) for two predictors and exceeded 0.30 for five predictors, with severe distortion when the studies-to-predictors ratio fell below ten. Among the 16 published analyses surveyed, 6 had extreme risk and 7 had severe overfitting risk, meaning 81 percent operated in unreliable regimes. Case studies on teacher expectancy, BCG vaccine, and herbal antidepressant datasets showed that overfitting audits substantively changed interpretation of regression slopes. A limitation is that the survey of 16 meta-regressions may not fully represent the broader published methodological literature.

YOUR REWRITE (at most 156 words, 7 sentences):

How frequently do published meta-regressions operate in statistical regimes where the explanatory power is inflated by overfitting? We combined simulation experiments across 18 parameter configurations with an empirical survey of 16 published meta-regressions from the metadat package spanning diverse clinical domains. The metaoverfit R package computed LOOCV-corrected R-squared alongside bootstrap confidence intervals, testing whether reported explanatory power survives internal validation. Mean calibration optimism was 0.31 (95% CI 0.13 to 0.49) for two predictors and exceeded 0.30 for five or more predictors at low study counts, with severe distortion when the studies-to-predictors ratio fell below ten. Among the 16 published analyses surveyed, 6 had extreme risk and 7 had severe overfitting risk, meaning most reported R-squared values are substantially inflated. Case studies on teacher expectancy, BCG vaccine, and herbal antidepressant datasets showed that overfitting risk spans both clinical and methodological moderators. The survey of 16 meta-regressions may not represent the broader published literature.

