How do treatment rankings in network meta-analysis change when transported to a target population with different baseline characteristics? We simulated 20 trials comparing four treatments with age and BMI as effect modifiers generating study-level aggregate data with known treatment-covariate interactions. The nmatransport R package implements transitivity assessment via covariate distance matrices, entropy balancing to reweight studies toward a target distribution, and transported NMA with adjusted standard errors. Standard NMA ranked treatment A optimal with P-score 0.85 while transported NMA for an older higher-BMI population ranked treatment C optimal with P-score 0.91 (95% CI 0.82 to 0.96). Transitivity diagnostics confirmed covariate imbalance and entropy-balanced weights reduced standardized mean differences below 0.05 for both modifiers across allirection-of-effect differences among studies contributing to different meta-analyses.

