docs\fix_ledger.json:6105:     "corpus_line": "MEASUREMENT topics (21, sealed before the engine existed): audit-found positives found 1 of 12 (named); pooled-or-declared positives found 120 of 135; 5 of 21 topics RAN_ERROR (the engine refused its own query; contribute 0 found, counted in N); sealed regression register 18 of 20 -- measured with the LEGACY concept-query engine (harness/acquisition.py, re-run because that file changed), NOT with search_v2, which has not been run against the sealed register; development topics excluded",
docs\fix_ledger.json:6109:      "corpus_line": "MEASUREMENT topics (21, sealed before the engine existed), run r2 (engine 9cf898d02afb, snapshot 2026-09-15r2-search_v2, guard protocol registered at 8d7cf8fc66ef): audit-found positives found 1 of 12 (named); pooled-or-declared positives found 135 of 135; topic states: RAN_OK 0 of 21; RAN_OK_WITH_SOURCE_ERRORS 21 of 21; RAN_ZERO 0 of 21; RAN_ERROR 0 of 21; NOT_RUN 0 of 21; source-level RAN_ERROR 163 of 837 sources across the 21; sealed regression register ON search_v2: within kind (PubMed concept query alone) 19 of 20 over 5 of 5 topics, whole engine 20 of 20 over 5 of 5 topics; development topics excluded"
docs\fair_judge.json:13:      "Mixed estimands pooled on one log scale and labelled 'mixed (HR/RR)' or 'mixed (HR/IRR/RR)': finerenone (FIDELIO as count-derived RR + FIGARO as HR), omega3, spironolactone (RALES RR + EMPHASIS HR). Declared, but a reader cannot audit a single named estimand.",
docs\fair_judge.json:54:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:59:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:66:    "verdict": "First (ours) is materially more auditable: verbatim queries, per-number quoted spans, per-record exclusion reasons, per-outcome declared absences. Second (comparator) is the more complete synthesis for harms and uses full-text extraction and human RoB; on mortality the two are at parity in k with disjoint modern trials on our side. Ours has two self-consistency defects a reader would catch (retraction N=3 vs k=5; RoB table missing SPLIT).",
docs\fair_judge.json:70:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:77:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:108:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:113:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:120:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable at the number and record level; first (comparator) is the more complete and better risk-of-bias-reported synthesis. The pooled estimates disagree in direction of significance (0.62 significant vs 0.82 non-significant), driven by ours excluding post-PVI and open-control trials and adding COP-AF; a reader can trace why on our page, not on theirs.",
docs\fair_judge.json:124:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:131:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:162:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:167:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:174:    "verdict": "First (ours) is more auditable and, for the renal composite, pools exactly the same two trials. Second (comparator) is the more complete review (harms, UACR, eGFR) and is registered, but its text contains two numerical self-contradictions. Ours' mixed HR/RR pooling and the 1-of-2 RoB coverage are its own audit findings.",
docs\fair_judge.json:178:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:185:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:216:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:221:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:228:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable on every reporting dimension except completeness; first (comparator) is far more complete and is the only one of the two that actually searched. The pooled MACE estimates agree (0.85 vs 0.86) with the same method, so the difference here is auditability and scope, not result.",
docs\fair_judge.json:232:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:239:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:270:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:275:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:282:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is the more auditable document by a wide margin, and is unusually candid about what it did not pool. First (comparator) is a much larger synthesis (28 trials, 5 outcomes) whose inputs cannot be checked from the text. Ours' three inconsistent statements of the comparator's k on one page is a reportable defect.",
docs\fair_judge.json:286:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:293:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:324:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:329:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:337:    "verdict": "First (ours) is more auditable; second (comparator) is a far more complete and registered review. With k=2 ours is a two-trial summary whose HKSJ interval is uninformative; the comparator's pooled RR 0.83 (0.79-0.87) rests on inputs a reader cannot see in the text.",
docs\fair_judge.json:341:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:348:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:379:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:384:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:391:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable and is honest about its scope (EPHESUS refused by preregistered population rule); first (comparator) is the more complete synthesis but its text contains population-label and scope contradictions that undercut its own auditability. Ours' RoB table covers 1 of 2 pooled trials and the pooled estimand is mixed.",
docs\fair_judge.json:395:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:402:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:433:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:438:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:445:    "verdict": "First (ours) is fully auditable but is a one-trial summary; second (comparator) is a large network meta-analysis whose every input lives in a supplement. The two estimates for denosumab vs placebo are close (0.32 vs 0.30/0.33), but only ours can be checked from the page, and ours' RR/HR labelling on the secondary rows is a defect.",
docs\fair_judge.json:449:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:456:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:461:    "more_auditable_tally": {
docs\fair_judge.json:495:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:501:    "honest_reading": "Ours wins auditability on all 8 topics and loses completeness on all 8. The comparators are registered (5 of 8: colchicine INPLASY; finerenone, pcsk9, spironolactone, denosumab PROSPERO), run genuine multi-database searches, and pool 1.1x to 55x more trials. Ours' pages carry self-consistency defects that an auditor would flag (subset RoB tables, retraction-line N != k, mixed estimands, three comparator-k values on the omega3 page, RR/HR label on denosumab secondary rows, 'PARITY' labels on pcsk9 and spironolactone where the comparator pooled 12 and 3 trials respectively)."
docs\fair_judge.json:516:        "reason": "First says 'change-from-baseline means and SDs were extracted where available' and summarizes the forest plot as 'Pooled estimate is minus two point ninety-nine with confidence interval minus five point ten to minus zero point eighty-nine', but the per-trial mean/SD inputs are not text-auditable. Second gives each pooled input with its source, e.g. 'mean -21.4 (SD 12.32, n=101) ... vs -17.0 (SD 13.88, n=100)' and 'mean -10.0 (SD 12.74, n=63) ... vs -6.3 (SD 8.86, n=60)'."
docs\fair_judge.json:530:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:532:        "reason": "Second is easier to audit end to end because the reader can see exact queries, every screening decision, every declared absence, and the two mean/SD source spans. First is stronger evidence but less directly auditable from the text because strategies, trial-level RoB synthesis, and some inputs live in supplements/figures. Deductions against second: primary RoB coverage is 0/2 and the method table still says 'log ratio' on a continuous MD page."
docs\fair_judge.json:535:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:542:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable, not more complete. It exposes the two pooled per-arm mean/SD inputs and every refusal, so a reader can reproduce why the pool is small and why the CI is wide. First (comparator) is the stronger evidence synthesis, with four acute MADRS trials and many efficacy/safety outcomes, but its judged text relies on supplements and figures for important audit details. The stale 'log ratio' method phrase and 0/2 RoB coverage are real defects in our page.",
docs\fair_judge.json:546:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:553:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:584:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:586:        "reason": "Second is more auditable because the one effect can be traced to a CT.gov mean/SD row and every exclusion/absence is visible. First is far more complete, but its per-trial sleep-latency inputs are in a forest plot and underlying data are 'available from the corresponding author by request'. Deductions against second: this is a k=1 trial summary, not a real meta-analysis, and the RoB section only declares non-assessment."
docs\fair_judge.json:589:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:596:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable but much thinner. Its single Wade/Circadin input is source-traced and the ten absences are declared, which makes the small result honest and checkable. First (comparator) is the stronger synthesis for evidence breadth, with a 15-trial sleep-latency pool and moderator analyses, but the judged text does not expose the forest-plot inputs or trial-level risk-of-bias judgements. This is the starkest auditability-versus-completeness split in the batch.",
docs\fair_judge.json:600:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:607:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:638:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:643:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:651:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable because it enforces and documents a single Week-68, treatment-policy mean-difference estimand and exposes each source row and refusal. First (comparator) is stronger on evidence breadth and even prints good per-trial mean/SD and safety tables, so this is not a weak comparator. The key methodological difference is that first pools a broader four-trial set, while second refuses O'Neil/Rubino as out of scope and drops two retrieved regional trials for Week-44 timepoint mismatch; our resulting k=2 interval is honest but very wide. The stale 'log ratio' method phrase and 0/2 RoB coverage remain page defects.",
docs\fair_judge.json:655:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:662:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:693:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:698:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:705:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable: it prints the exact local searches, every pooled input, every declared absence, and every exclusion/refusal reason. The comparator is more complete and clinically broader, with 5 trials, safety outcomes, and human review, but its primary forest plot and full risk-of-bias display are structurally absent from the judged text. The k gap is honest rather than hidden: ours is narrower and smaller because it enforces double-blind/placebo design and excludes open-label or postpericardiotomy-scope records.",
docs\fair_judge.json:709:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:716:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:747:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:752:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:759:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable for the stated empagliflozin HFpEF/HFmrEF question: the single pivotal trial, exclusions, declared absences, and source count are all visible and replayable. The comparator is stronger as a broad SGLT2 class review and is more complete for that different PICO, but its larger k is mostly scope expansion rather than missing empagliflozin evidence. Ours is therefore smaller and less clinically expansive, while also clearer about why it is small.",
docs\fair_judge.json:763:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:770:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:801:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:803:        "reason": "First says '168 of 168 numerical claims' have a one-click source and documents the 15-vs-42 gap; second is far more complete and includes human RoB/GRADE, but key audit surfaces (forest plots, supplemental search terms, and detailed RoB figure) are absent from the text-only extraction."
docs\fair_judge.json:806:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:813:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable: exact searches, source rows, declared absences, exclusion spans, and the gap decomposition are all in the page. The comparator is much more complete and methodologically richer for adult AAD, especially on RoB/GRADE and subgroup analysis, and that matters because ours pools only 15 of a much larger published evidence base. Ours partially explains the smaller k through stricter definitions and ineligibility decisions, but the remaining reach/recovery targets mean the comparator is stronger on completeness.",
docs\fair_judge.json:817:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:824:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:855:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:860:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:867:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable for the HFrEF primary comparison because the same-scope two-trial pool is explicit, source rows are visible, and every exclusion is replayable. The comparator is more complete as an all-heart-failure SGLT2 review, with secondary outcomes, subgroup analyses, and stronger human RoB reporting. Ours is smaller and its HKSJ k=2 interval is much wider, but the page is clearer about which evidence is in scope and why the other comparator trials are out of scope.",
docs\fair_judge.json:871:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:878:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:909:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:914:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:921:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable because the treatment-PICO search, WOMAN estimate, declared absences, and prophylaxis exclusions are all visible and replayable. The comparator is plainly more complete and clinically authoritative: it uses IPD/aggregate data from five large trials and reports many outcomes with RoB/GRADE. The important limitation is that ours is deliberately much narrower and smaller, and it does not reproduce the comparator's prophylaxis-plus-treatment breadth or its IPD safety analyses.",
docs\fair_judge.json:925:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:932:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:957:        "reason": "Second renders auditable RoB fields and limits: 'D1/D2/D4 use AACT structured allocation/masking fields' and 'D3... marked not assessed - requires human judgement'. First says it used the Cochrane tool and that 'Figure 2 demonstrates the methodological quality', but the actual RoB plot is absent from the judged text."
docs\fair_judge.json:961:        "reason": "Second gives a flow plus per-record audit trail: 'Excluded at screening - by rule 92 (X1 62 · X2 16 · X3 14)' and 'Every excluded record's rule id, reason and verbatim span are listed', plus 26 declared-absent outcome rows. First gives aggregate exclusions after screening but not record-level reasons in the judged text."
docs\fair_judge.json:963:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:968:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:975:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is clearly more auditable: exact queries, committed cache, replay status, per-number source spans, per-record exclusion reasons, and declared absences are all in the judged text. First (comparator) is the more complete clinical synthesis, with many more RCTs and outcomes, but the text-only extraction hides the forest-plot inputs, detailed search supplement, and RoB plot needed to audit the numbers at source level.",
docs\fair_judge.json:979:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:986:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1017:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1022:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:1029:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable because the judged text exposes replay, cache, exact screening decisions, declared absences, and source spans for every pooled number. First (comparator) is materially more complete and better on human risk-of-bias reporting, but the text-only file does not expose the detailed search supplement or per-trial forest-plot inputs needed to audit the pooled ORs directly.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1033:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1040:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1065:        "reason": "Second renders auditable RoB fields for its included trial and states the limits: 'D1/D2/D4 use AACT structured allocation/masking fields' and 'D3... marked not assessed'. First says Cochrane RoB was applied and 'all trials met the criteria for low risk of bias (Figure S1)', but Figure S1 and per-domain judgements are absent from the judged text."
docs\fair_judge.json:1071:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1073:        "reason": "Second exposes deterministic replay ('Reproduction census failures 0', 'Replayed offline from committed cache True') and exact structured event counts. First is more complete but is a class-level network meta-analysis; its network inputs, Figure S1 RoB details, and supplement/table machinery are not auditable from this text-only extraction."
docs\fair_judge.json:1076:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:1083:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable for the exact sacubitril/valsartan question: event counts, source, absences, exclusions, cache, and replay are inspectable. First (comparator) is much more complete as a broader class-level network meta-analysis and reports the same directional ARNI-vs-RASi conclusion, but it is less directly auditable from the text extraction and is not a clean single-agent comparator.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1087:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1094:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1125:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1127:        "reason": "Second is more auditable: 'Reproduction census failures 0', 'Replayed offline from committed cache True', and '14 of 14 numerical claims... carry a one-click source'. First is larger and richer on observational quality grading, but its search supplement and forest-plot per-study inputs are absent from the judged text, and it is not an RCT comparator."
docs\fair_judge.json:1130:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:1137:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable and better aligned to a randomized statin-vs-control harness: it exposes exact queries, source spans, declared absences, per-record exclusions, and replay. First (comparator) is more complete only as an observational synthesis, with far larger N and richer quality grading, but it explicitly uses observational studies and excludes clinical trials, so its larger k is not a like-for-like RCT advantage.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1141:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1148:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1179:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1181:        "reason": "Second is much easier to audit end to end because its query strings, cache path, source span, declared absences, exclusion rules, protocol SHA and replay hash are in the text. First is far more complete and reports Cochrane RoB summaries, but in this text-only extraction the search strategy, per-trial inputs, figures and supplementary detail needed to recheck the meta-analysis are absent."
docs\fair_judge.json:1184:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:1191:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable but much less complete: it exposes the exact searches, one source-traced RECOVERY input, every exclusion, every declared absence, and deterministic replay metadata. The comparator is the stronger evidence synthesis, using a prospective 7-trial COVID-19 corticosteroid collaboration and investigator-supplied 28-day mortality data that are not in the text ours can verify. The main caveat against the comparator in this lane is structural: the judged comparator file is a text-only extraction, so forest plots, supplementary tables and any full search details are absent.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1195:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1202:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1233:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1235:        "reason": "Second is more auditable for the exact claim it makes: the one pooled number, every absence, every exclusion, protocol SHA, cache path and replay state are directly checkable. First is much stronger on systematic-review method, search appendices, RoB and GRADE, but its pooled ovulation inputs are not source-quoted in the judged text and forest/supplement-style material remains structurally harder to audit from the text extraction alone."
docs\fair_judge.json:1238:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:1245:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable for its own narrow, source-traced claim, but the comparator is a substantially fuller Cochrane review and is stronger on search, RoB and certainty assessment. The cost of ours' auditability is severe incompleteness: it reduces a 13-study ovulation evidence base to one abstract-extractable trial and declares the rest absent. The honest reading is that ours is a transparent audit harness, not a replacement for the full Cochrane evidence synthesis.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1249:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1256:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1287:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1289:        "reason": "Second is more auditable for the single-agent SELECT claim because its query, source text, registry corroboration, exclusions, protocol SHA and replay hash are visible. First is broader and methodologically useful, but the full search algorithms, exclusion list, detailed RoB figure and forest-plot inputs are structurally absent from the text-only comparator extraction."
docs\fair_judge.json:1292:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:1299:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable for semaglutide-alone MACE in obesity without diabetes because the SELECT number and the screening decisions are directly traceable. The comparator is more complete only by asking a broader class question: GLP-1 RAs and GIP/GLP-1 agents rather than semaglutide alone. That broader document is clinically informative and richer on outcomes, but its search algorithms, exclusions, detailed RoB figure and per-trial forest-plot inputs are absent from the judged text.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1303:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1310:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1341:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1343:        "reason": "First is more auditable because a reader can trace the one MACE input, the bleeding input, exclusions, declared absence, protocol SHA and replay hash directly. Second is broader but harder to audit from the text-only extraction because S1 Fig, the forest-plot per-study inputs and detailed RoB figures are absent, and its broader review mixes ticagrelor-vs-clopidogrel with ticagrelor-vs-prasugrel and stable-CAD records."
docs\fair_judge.json:1346:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:1353:    "verdict": "Ours is more auditable: the PLATO MACE effect and CT.gov bleeding counts are visible, and every screening exclusion and declared absence is documented. The comparator is more complete and outcome-rich, but it is less clean for this PICO because the judged text blends clopidogrel and prasugrel comparisons and includes broader CAD/DM material, while the search details, forest plots and RoB figures are absent from the text extraction. The fair conclusion is parity or near-parity for the core PLATO-driven MACE claim, with the comparator stronger for breadth and ours stronger for traceable audit.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1357:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1364:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1395:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1397:        "reason": "First is replayable from a cache, shows exact queries, per-number source spans, exclusion reasons, declared absences and a protocol SHA. Second is more complete for the broader SGLT2 class, but in the text-only extraction the search details, PRISMA flow, RoB table and some per-trial provenance are in figures/supplements rather than auditable text."
docs\fair_judge.json:1400:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:1407:    "verdict": "First (ours) is more auditable: exact queries, source-spanned numbers, per-record exclusions, declared absences and replay metadata are in the judged text. Second (comparator) is stronger and more complete as a class-level SGLT2 synthesis, but it is not a like-for-like dapagliflozin-only comparator; its larger k is mostly scope breadth, and the text-only extraction lacks its figures/supplementary tables.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1411:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1418:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1449:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1454:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:1461:    "verdict": "First (ours) is more auditable and reaches near-identical primary results to the comparator (0.805 vs 0.81) from source-visible trial effects. Second (comparator) is the stronger scientific synthesis because it uses IPD, harmonized follow-up and many more outcomes/subgroups, but its decisive inputs live in restricted data and absent supplements rather than the judged text.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1465:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1472:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1503:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1505:        "reason": "Second lets a reader audit exact queries, every pooled input, every declared absence and the k=3 vs k=10 gap rationale. First is far more complete, but the cached text is largely abstract/metadata: it reports aggregate results and SMART-C criteria without per-trial inputs, exclusions or RoB details."
docs\fair_judge.json:1508:    "more_auditable": "B",
docs\fair_judge.json:1515:    "verdict": "Second (ours) is more auditable because its k=3 estimate, exclusions, declared absences and source spans are visible. First (comparator) is plainly more complete and likely more informative scientifically, with 10 trials and broad subgroup/outcome coverage; the fair caveat is that the cached comparator is mostly text-only abstract/metadata, so its superiority in completeness is not matched by source-level auditability in the judged text.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1519:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1526:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
docs\fair_judge.json:1557:      "overall_auditability": {
docs\fair_judge.json:1562:    "more_auditable": "A",
docs\fair_judge.json:1570:    "verdict": "First (ours) is more auditable but much less complete: it gives a transparent RECOVERY-only mortality estimate and names the extraction limits, while second (comparator) is a larger class-level IL-6 antagonist meta-analysis with tocilizumab subgroup results and more outcomes. The honest reading is that ours is source-checkable but small and strict; the comparator is scientifically broader but, in this text-only cache, less independently auditable at the per-trial level.",
docs\fair_judge.json:1574:      "more_auditable": "ours",
docs\fair_judge.json:1581:        "overall_auditability": "ours"
topics\sacubitril-valsartan-hfref.json:21:  "comparator_scope_note": "k=1 CONTRIBUTING, not a complete-evidence claim: PARADIGM-HF supplies the only EXTRACTABLE primary-outcome estimate here; 7 records screened in and 6 are declared absent for this pooled number. This is NOT the complete eligible evidence base -- external audit identified PARALLEL-HF (225 HFrEF patients, sacubitril/valsartan vs enalapril, the same primary composite) as a further eligible RCT the trial-level search missed; the concept-query search rebuild is the fix. Separately, the only open-access comparator (PMID 36722326) is a multi-class network meta-analysis, not a sacubitril-specific pairwise meta (a scope mismatch, stated). Component/harm secondaries were dropped rather than shipped ambiguous (see refusals).",
docs\search_test_set.json:2: "_doc": "Named missing-trial test set for SEARCH RECALL -- the eligible trials the adversarial audits identified as missing from each topic's pool, each tagged with the RECOVERY MECHANISM that should retrieve it. Unaided recall against this set is the project's headline search metric: no published meta-analysis reports a cold-start recall number against a named adversarial test set about itself. Search is MEASURED against this, never declared 'solved'. Identifiers marked pending are under external verification (CT.gov/PubMed/EuropePMC) by a read-only session; a trial's presence here does not assert its PMID, it asserts the audit judged it eligible-and-missing.",
docs\search_test_set.json:17:   "trial": "SONIA",
