his topic.</div><h4>Positive-control recovery (NOT systematic-review recall)</h4><p>Positive-control recovery: the committed registry query re-found <strong>1/1</strong> of this topic's PRE-SPECIFIED known trials (pooled + declared positive controls; enumerated 126; status RAN_OK). <span class='muted'>Measured 2026-09-11T23:39:14Z.</span> <strong>This is not systematic-review recall.</strong> It measures whether the committed registry query re-finds the trials ALREADY KNOWN to the build; a trial that was never in the known set is not in the denominator, so a high value does <strong>not</strong> mean the search is complete ? external audits found eligible trials (J-EMPHASIS-HF, an eplerenone trial, for the MRA topic published under the identifier spironolactone-hfref-mortality, PHILO for ticagrelor) entirely absent precisely because they were never in a known set. True recall needs an INDEPENDENTLY-GENERATED reference universe (concept query + registry enumeration, not the seed list); that rebuild is in progress. Recovery is also search REACH, not inclusion — whether a recovered trial is eligible/poolable is the screen's and extractor's job.</p><h4>Registry landscape &amp; unpublished evidence (AACT)</h4><p>Of <strong>126</strong> registry records matching the query (broad — reach, not precision): 64 have a linked publication; <strong>10</strong> have posted CT.gov results but no publication (<em>poolable unpublished data no published meta in this topic has</em>); 25 are completed &ge;12 months ago with neither results nor a linked publication — a <em>loose upper bound</em> on non-publication, inflated by the broad enumeration and by NCT&rarr;PMID linkage misses, not a publication-bias claim. <span class='muted'>AACT 2026-08-30 (local snapshot).</span></p><h4>PubMed</h4><pre class='query'>19717846[uid]</pre><pre class='query'>17980250[uid]</pre><h4>ClinicalTrials.gov</h4><pre class='query'>{&quot;cond&quot;: &quot;STEMI fibrinolysis&quot;, &quot;intr&quot;: &quot;ticagrelor&quot;}</pre></section><section class="tab" id="tab-screening"><h3 class="tabname">Screening</h3><p class='muted'><strong>Identifier scope:</strong> identifier names ticagrelor; all included records map to that agent. Verdict: <code>MATCH</code>.</p><h4>Study selection flow (PRISMA 2020)</h4><table class='recs'><tr><th>Stage</th><th>n</th></tr><tr><td>Records identified (committed search)</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Records screened (deduplicated)</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Excluded at screening — by rule</td><td>29 (X-DESIGN 6 · X1 15 · X2 6 · X3 2)</td></tr><tr><td>Met eligibility (P/I/C/design)</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pooled in the primary outcome (k)</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Eligible but outcome not extracted from the abstract (full-text pass pending)</td><td>1</td></tr></table><p class='note'>Every excluded record's rule id, reason and verbatim span are listed below (PRISMA item 16b: exclusions with reasons).</p><h4>Dual independent screening (PRISMA item 8)</h4><p>Two independently-implemented rule screeners over 32 records: agreement <strong>29/32</strong>, disagreement <strong>9.4%</strong> (3 records). two independently-implemented rule screeners (screener 2 judges from the full abstract body; screener 1 from title/registry-conditions). Adjudicator: screener 1. <em>the two rule sets share an author and the same eligibility criteria, so they are NOT statistically independent; this agreement overstates inter-rater reliability. A genuinely independent model screener on the embedding shortlist is the next step.</em></p><h5>Independent model adjudication of the disagreements</h5><p>A capable model (different information + method than the two correlated rule sets) adjudicated 2 content-bearing disagreements; it agrees with the served rule screener on <strong>2/2</strong>. an independent capable-model reader adjudicated the rule-screener disagreements (different information + method than the two correlated rule sets).